Adjustable / fill-pointer character vectors are %MD-ARRAY, not recognised as STRING
==================================================================================

SYMPTOM
-------
An adjustable and/or fill-pointer vector of characters is classified by bocl
as %MD-ARRAY, and STRINGP / (TYPEP x 'STRING) / CLASS-OF all say it is NOT a
string, even though it is a rank-1 character vector:

    (let ((a (make-array 3 :element-type 'character :adjustable t :fill-pointer 0)))
      (vector-push-extend #\a a) (vector-push-extend #\b a)
      (list (type-of a) (stringp a) (typep a 'string) (vectorp a)))
    => (COMMON-LISP::%MD-ARRAY NIL NIL T)      ; want STRINGP / TYPEP-STRING = T

Per CLHS a STRING is *any* one-dimensional array whose element type is a
subtype of CHARACTER — adjustable and fill-pointer strings included.  bocl
only treats its *simple* string representation (STRING_Class, octet_array
storage) as a string; every complex 1-D char array is the generic %MD-ARRAY.

CONSEQUENCES (why this matters)
-------------------------------
1. STRINGP / TYPEP: false negatives for adjustable strings.
2. CLOS dispatch: a method specialised on ((x string)) is NOT selected for an
   adjustable char vector, because %APPLICABLE-P tests built-in specializers
   with kernel TYPEP (prelude %applicable-p -> *%kernel-typep*).  The arg
   falls through to the default method.  This is exactly what breaks cl-ppcre
   (see bug-cl-ppcre.issue): its regex parser accumulates multi-character
   literal runs into an ADJUSTABLE string (parser.lisp SEQ /
   make-array-from-two-chars + vector-push-extend), so a run like the literal
   \E converts via CONVERT-SIMPLE-PARSE-TREE's *default* method (not the
   ((parse-tree string)) method) and signals  "Unknown token \E in parse tree."
3. COERCE, string kernel ops: worked around piecemeal (COERCE now uses
   ELT/LENGTH — v1.9.3), but the general defect remains.

ROOT CAUSE
----------
sources/kernel.c:  bool stringp(Ref o){ return class_of(o)==STRING_Class; }
STRING_Class is the simple-string built-in class only; adjustable char arrays
have class %MD-ARRAY.  The kernel string primitives (string_length,
string_octets, string_cstring, string_eq, char accessors, ...) all
check_class(x, STRING_Class) and so cannot operate on an %MD-ARRAY either.

FIX (design — larger than a point fix)
--------------------------------------
Unify complex 1-D character arrays with strings at the type-system level:
 - STRINGP and TYPEP against STRING must be true for a rank-1 %MD-ARRAY whose
   element-type is a subtype of CHARACTER (this alone fixes CLOS dispatch,
   since %APPLICABLE-P routes through TYPEP).
 - The string kernel primitives must then accept such arrays (read/scan their
   element storage), OR bocl must grow a real adjustable-string representation
   that is a subclass of STRING (preferred: CLASS-OF returns a string class so
   PRINT-OBJECT / TYPE-OF / methods are all consistent).
Do it as a deliberate change with its own tests (STRINGP/TYPEP/CLOS-dispatch/
CHAR/AREF/LENGTH/COERCE/PRINT on adjustable + fill-pointer strings), because
the surface is wide.

Discovered 2026-07-23 while fixing bug-cl-ppcre (two prerequisite bugs — a LOOP
`for v = init then step` stepping bug and a COERCE %MD-ARRAY bug — were fixed in
v1.9.3; this is the remaining blocker for loading cl-ppcre).

====================================================================
RESOLUTION (v1.9.4, 2026-07-24)

Fixed at the Lisp/type-system layer (a %MD-ARRAY is a Lisp struct, so the
recognition lives above the kernel):

* %MD-STRING-P + a wrapped STRINGP: a rank-1 character %MD-ARRAY is now a
  STRING.
* %TYPEP-BUILTIN wraps the kernel TYPEP with the complex-array cases it
  misses — a %MD-ARRAY satisfies ARRAY; a rank-1 one VECTOR / SEQUENCE and,
  by element type, STRING / BASE-STRING or BIT-VECTOR (never SIMPLE-*).  Both
  TYPEP definitions and the CLOS applicability tests (%APPLICABLE-P,
  %SPECIALIZER-POSITION) route through it, so (TYPEP x 'STRING) and a method
  specialised on ((x string)) now apply to a complex string — matching the
  ARRAYP / VECTORP / STRINGP predicate wrappers (previously TYPEP disagreed
  with them).
* Element access: CHAR / SCHAR / (SETF CHAR) / STRING-LENGTH taught to follow
  a %MD-ARRAY (row-major storage), so every string function layered on them
  (SUBSEQ, case ops, %COERCE-TO-CHARACTER, …) works on adjustable / displaced
  / fill-pointer strings.

Covered by the "adjustable string is string" case in tests/test_conformance.c;
full CUnit suite green.  This unblocked the CLOS-dispatch failure in
bug-cl-ppcre (which now advances to an unrelated (SETF (THE …) …) gap).
Note: the kernel string primitives (string_length, string_octets, …) still
require a simple kernel STRING — callers reach complex strings through the
%MD-ARRAY-aware Lisp wrappers, which is the existing design.
